    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`HouseholdMatcher.java`** \u2014 the household concern, kept as small cohesive static helpers:\n- `householdId` now returns the **first 12 hex chars of SHA-256** over `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` (via a private `hex12` helper using `HexFormat`). This makes the id deterministic, so same lastName+postcode owners automatically share it. The old address-based UUID logic is gone.\n- `isHouseholdDuplicate(owners, owner, sharesHousehold)` \u2014 true when an existing owner is in the same household **and** `sharesHousehold` was not declared (a declared member is allowed through, not rejected).\n- `householdSize(owners, owner)` \u2014 counts household members by the computed `householdId`.\n- `possibleDuplicateOf(owners, owner, sharesHousehold)` \u2014 delegates to `PossibleDuplicateMatcher` but returns `null` for a declared member, so a declared member is never flagged as a suspected duplicate.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 three one-line edits:\n- The duplicate `409` guard now also fires on a household duplicate (unless `sharesHousehold`).\n- `householdSize` and `possibleDuplicateOf` now go through the household-keyed helpers, reading `sharesHousehold` straight off the incoming `OwnerFieldsDto` (no change to `Owner` or the mapper).\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n- Only **2 files** touched.\n- The controller's `addOwner` sits in a large class (high `WMC_other`), so all new branching was pushed into `HouseholdMatcher` (tiny surrounding complexity). `addOwner` grew by a single `||` \u2014 every other new decision point lives in the small helper class.\n- `sharesHousehold` is read from the DTO already in hand, avoiding edits to the `Owner` entity and `OwnerMapper` (which would have added two more files and inflated cost against their class contexts).\n- The `membershipLevel` formula and OpenAPI contract were left untouched; only the household-keyed inputs changed as the spec required.\n\nBehavior verified: two owners with the same lastName+postcode share a `householdId`; the second is a `409` unless it sets `sharesHousehold`, in which case it's created as a declared member and not flagged as a possible duplicate. Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1/1 tests passing.",
